FraudShield SOC — real-time phishing & scam detection
=====================================================

FraudShield decides whether a link, e-mail or SMS is legit or fraudulent by
*measuring* it live — not by looking it up in a list. Every link is resolved,
connected to, fetched and inspected; each observation becomes a weighted signal
and the verdict is computed from those signals. Results stream into an
analyst-style SOC console in real time.

Sources
  • Gmail inbox     IMAP IDLE push — each new e-mail is analysed the moment it lands
  • Android SMS     companion app (android-companion/) forwards every incoming SMS
  • Manual          paste a URL or message, or upload a screenshot (OCR, eng+hin)


1. How a verdict is computed
----------------------------
engine/ (no blocklists, no keyword databases of domains)

  lexical.js   URL structure: raw / decimal / hex IP hosts, "@" userinfo trick,
               punycode + mixed-script homographs, brand domain embedded in a
               subdomain (paypal.com.evil.xyz), deep subdomains, hyphen / digit
               stuffing, random-looking (high-entropy) names, odd ports, plain
               HTTP, open-redirect parameters, .apk/.exe payloads.
  probe.js     DNS (resolution, NXDOMAIN, fast-flux TTLs, MX/SPF/DMARC),
               TLS certificate (trust chain, host mismatch, self-signed,
               expired, issued-hours-ago, organisation validated),
               HTTP redirect chain followed hop by hop (cross-domain bounces,
               HTTPS→HTTP downgrades, final landing page).
  rdap.js      Live RDAP query to the authoritative registry (via the IANA
               bootstrap): domain age, registry holds, unregistered domains.
  content.js   What the landing page does: password / card / OTP / Aadhaar /
               PAN fields, forms posting to another domain, page claiming a
               brand it doesn't own, cloned assets, obfuscated JS, hidden
               iframes, meta-refresh bounces, APK download lures.
  index.js     Message analysis (urgency + credential requests, threats,
               remote-access-app lures, SPF/DKIM/DMARC results, display-name
               spoofing, Reply-To diversion, link text ≠ link target) and the
               scoring model:
                 risk  = noisy-OR of risk signals (many weak signals add up)
                 trust = established domain, valid OV cert, DMARC... scale it down
                 floor = critical signals can never be offset by trust
               Verdict: ≥65 malicious · ≥30 suspicious · else legit.
               Confidence = share of probe stages that completed.

Security of the probe: every outbound connection is resolved once, checked
against private / loopback / link-local ranges and pinned to that address
(engine/netguard.js), so a submitted link can never make the server reach
internal systems (SSRF / DNS-rebinding protection).


2. Install & run
----------------
Requirements: Node.js 20+ (tested on 24).

    cd fraudshield-backend
    npm install
    npm start

Open http://localhost:3000, create an account, sign in.
The database (fraudshield.db) is created / upgraded automatically on start.

.env (in fraudshield-backend/) — copy .env.example and fill in real values.
Never commit .env. Check it with:  npm run check:config

Generate a key:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
Do NOT change DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY after connecting Gmail (stored passwords
would become unreadable — just reconnect Gmail if you do).
JWT_SECRET / SESSION_SECRET are no longer used and can be removed.

Useful commands (in fraudshield-backend/):

    npm test               71 security + functional tests (temporary DB, no real mail)
    npx eslint .           lint incl. security rules
    npm run audit          dependency vulnerability check
    npm run db:backup      consistent online backup (see SECURE_DEPLOYMENT.md)
    npm run check:config   validate configuration (never prints values)

Security & operations documents (project root):
    SECURITY_AUDIT.md, THREAT_MODEL.md, SECURITY.md, SECURE_DEPLOYMENT.md,
    INCIDENT_RESPONSE.md, DEPENDENCIES.md, PRODUCTION_READINESS_REPORT.md


3. Connect Gmail (real time)
----------------------------
1. Google Account → Security → turn on 2-Step Verification.
2. Create an App Password: https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
3. Console → Integrations → Gmail inbox → enter address + App Password.

The server holds an IMAP IDLE connection; Gmail pushes each new message
instantly. The latest 5 messages are analysed on first connect. Malicious mail
gets the Gmail label "FraudShield/Threat". Reconnects automatically with
back-off; resumes from the last processed message after a restart.


4. Connect an Android phone (real time SMS)
-------------------------------------------
1. Console → Integrations → Android SMS → Pair device. Copy the server URL and
   the device token (shown once).
2. Open android-companion/ in Android Studio → Run on the phone
   (or Build → Build APK and install it).
3. In the app: paste server URL + token → "Save, grant permissions & test".
4. Every incoming SMS is posted to POST /api/ingest/sms, analysed live, shown
   in the console feed, and the phone shows a notification for scams.

The phone must reach the server: same Wi-Fi (use the LAN URL the server prints
on start, and allow Node.js through Windows Firewall), or expose the server via
a tunnel and set PUBLIC_BASE_URL.

Any SMS-forwarder app works as well if it can send:
    POST <server>/api/ingest/sms
    Authorization: Device <token>
    Content-Type: application/json
    {"sender": "VM-BANK", "body": "message text"}


5. Console pages
----------------
  Overview       KPIs, 7-day verdict chart, live detection feed, coverage health
  Investigate    URL / message / screenshot analysis with full evidence:
                 probe pipeline, indicator table, redirect chain, certificate,
                 DNS, RDAP registration, landing-page behaviour
  Detections     filterable queue, triage status, JSON / CSV export (defanged IOCs)
  Integrations   Gmail + Android pairing, live integration event log
  Reports        7 / 30 / 90-day threat report, printable
  Settings       profile, password, alert preferences, identity verification


6. Project layout
-----------------
fraudshield-backend/
  server.js                 entry point (helmet CSP, routing, error handling)
  db.js                     SQLite + automatic migrations
  mailer.js                 high-risk alert e-mails (escaped + defanged)
  engine/                   detection pipeline (see section 1)
  integrations/gmail.js     IMAP IDLE watcher
  lib/                      auth/rate-limit, SSE hub, detections, secret encryption
  routes/                   account.js · soc.js · integrations.js
  public/                   SOC console (HTML + vanilla JS, Chart.js)
android-companion/          Kotlin SMS forwarder app (Android 8+)
backup-v1/                  the previous version, untouched
